T44 — why the SFU cannot be reached from this host (2026-10-27)

Everything below was run on MacBook-Pro-5.local (macOS 26.3.1, Xcode 26.6) against the
fixture server used by every live card in this programme:
  docker container mm-perf = mattermost/mattermost-team-edition:release-9 (9.11.19),
  Calls plugin com.mattermost.calls 0.29.8 (build 936dd00d3e456c843b846740aa76601d07e6a25a).

1) The Calls plugin is installed and active, and it is an SFU (server-side media), not a relay
   that lets participants connect to each other. From the pinned source of the installed build
   (mattermost-plugin-calls v0.29.8 = 936dd00):
     server/activate.go   -> rtc.NewServer(rtc.ServerConfig{ICEAddressUDP, ICEPortUDP: *cfg.UDPServerPort...})
     server/websocket.go  -> p.sendRTCMessage(...) -> p.rtcServer.Send(msg)
     server/websocket.go  -> wsWriter() publishes the SFU's own SDP/candidates as `signal`
   So every participant's media terminates on a peer connection owned by the server process.

2) That server process runs inside the mm-perf container, on the container's own network.
   $ docker port mm-perf
   8065/tcp -> 0.0.0.0:8065
   $ docker inspect mm-perf --format '{{.HostConfig.NetworkMode}} ...'
   mm-perf-net mm-perf-net=172.19.0.3

   The SFU's ICE listener is bound inside the container on 8443 (hex 20FB), local address
   030013AC = 172.19.0.3:
   $ docker exec mm-perf cat /proc/net/udp | head -8
     sl  local_address rem_address   st ...
   3492: 030013AC:20FB 00000000:0000 07 ...
   3492: 030013AC:20FB 00000000:0000 07 ...
   (six sockets, one per ICE group)
   No UDP or TCP port other than 8065/tcp is published to the host, so the host cannot reach
   172.19.0.3 at all, and Docker Desktop on macOS does not route container IPs.

3) The candidates the SFU advertised to a real client (captured by scripts/t44_calls_probe.py,
   run B, `signal` events after a MessagePack sdp offer):
     candidate:2878742611 1 udp 2130706431 127.0.0.1      8443 typ host
     candidate:774415737  1 udp 2130706431 64.251.85.226   8443 typ host
     candidate:375738803  1 tcp 1671430143 127.0.0.1      8443 typ host tcptype passive
     candidate:2480657561 1 tcp 1671430143 64.251.85.226   8443 typ host tcptype passive
     candidate:1244823927 1 udp 1694498815 209.35.187.178 53080 typ srflx raddr 0.0.0.0 rport 38030
   127.0.0.1 is the container's own loopback; 64.251.85.226 is the Docker Desktop VM's
   bridged address (the Mac itself is 172.21.24.65 on en0); 209.35.187.178 is this network's
   public address as reported by two public STUN servers.

4) Reachability, measured with a STUN binding request to each advertised UDP candidate:
   $ python3 scripts/t44_calls_probe.py --url http://127.0.0.1:8065   (tail)
     DATA  stun binding to 127.0.0.1:8443  —  no reply (2.0 s)
     DATA  stun binding to 64.251.85.226:8443  —  no reply (2.0 s)
     DATA  stun binding to 209.35.187.178:53080  —  no reply (2.0 s)
     DATA  control: a public STUN server answers from this host  —  yes
     MEDIA PATH: 0/3 advertised UDP candidate(s) reachable from this host — media cannot flow here (see the build note)

   The control line matters: the same probe, from the same host, gets a Binding Success from
   stun.l.google.com:19302 and from stun.global.calls.mattermost.com:3478 (the STUN server the
   plugin itself hands to clients), with xor-mapped-address = 209.35.187.178. So "no reply" is a
   fact about the port, not about the probe or the network path off this machine.

5) Consequence, stated plainly: on this fixture no client — native or web, on the host or on a
   device — can establish the media leg of a Calls session, because the SFU's ICE port is
   published nowhere. SIP-style signalling completes (join, offer, answer, candidates) and then
   ICE never connects.

What would change it (not done in T44 — it is a change to a shared fixture, not to the app):
  a) publish the SFU's port through a UDP relay sidecar on the same docker network, e.g.
       docker run -d --network mm-perf-net -p 8443:8443/udp alpine/socat \
         UDP4-LISTEN:8443,fork UDP4:mm-perf:8443
  b) set the plugin's ICEHostOverride to a host address that reaches that relay (127.0.0.1), via
     PUT /api/v4/plugins/com.mattermost.calls/config (changes are rejected while a call is active);
  c) re-run scripts/t44_calls_probe.py and require the reachability block to report replies —
     a reachable SFU is the hard precondition for any bidirectional-audio measurement.
